Ruler.; Dynastes.

@AE 783[3]. Hence those in falsities are called "rulers," etc.

Ruler of Feast.; Architriclinus.

@AE 376[29]. "The ruler of the feast" (John 2) = those in the Knowledges of truth.

Ruling love.; Amor dominans.
See <Reigning love>.

@AC 6690[2]. The disposition of scientific truths in the natural is varied with every man; for the ruling love induces a form on them. This love is in the middle, and ordinates every thing round itself, placing nearest itself the things which are most in agreement, and the rest in order, according to their agreement. Example(s).

@7081. The ruling or reigning love, that is, the love of the Thing which one has for the end, is what makes a man. This love has subordinated to it many particular and singular loves, which are derivations, and appear under another aspect, but still the ruling love is in each of them, and directs them, and, through them, as through mediate ends, regards and pursues its own end, which is the primary and ultimate end of all; and this both directly and indirectly.

@7648[end]. The ruling affection is the veriest form of the spirit, and all the rest of its affections apply themselves into it.

@8794[end]. Everyone (there) comes among those with whom he had communicated in the life of the body: his ruling love determines this; for this is what constitutes everyone's sphere of life, and it extends itself according to its quality and quantity.

@NJHD 54. The ruling love is like the king and head (of all the other loves.)

@58. Everyone has all his delight and happiness from his ruling love, and according to it...

@62. That all the interiors... are disposed into a form according to the ruling love. References.

Rumah.; Reumah.

@AC 2868. "His concubine, and her name was Rumah" (Genesis 22:24) = the Gentiles who are in idolatrous worship and in good.

@--[end]. That her name is called Rumah, involves the quality thereof; here, exaltation, which is meant by "Rumah."

Ruminate.; Ruminare.
Ruminatory.; Ruminatorius.

@AC 5135[2]. When man... begins to think for himself, he... as it were ruminates the things he had before learned and believed...

@DP 233[8]. The memory of man may be compared to the ruminatory stomach of some animals... and, so long as the food is there, it is not in their bodies, but outside of them; but, as they take it out thence, and devour it, it becomes of their life... Spiritual food is Knowledges, and in proportion as man takes these out (of his memory) by thinking of, and as it were ruminating them, his spiritual mind is nourished. @TCR 173.

@CL 326[3]. He said, I have meditated and ruminated about this distinction...

@TCR 146[2]. While they are preaching and teaching, they hang (the bag into which they have thrown faith and charity) before them as a kind of ruminatory stomach, from which they take out and disgorge such things as they know will serve for food to their hearers.

@SE 2018. Upon which he ruminates...

@AE 242[4]. Illustrated by comparison with the stomachs possessed by birds and animals of the earth, which are called ruminatory stomachs... The memory corresponds to these stomachs... into this, man first collects spiritual foods, which are Knowledges, and afterwards takes them out by as it were ruminating, that is, thinking and willing, them... @290[end].

Rumour.; Rumor.

@AC 3353[2]. "Wars and rumours of wars" (Matthew 24:6). ( = discussions and disputes about truths, and the consequent falsifications. @AE 734-24.)

Run.; Currere.
Run about.; Discurrere.
Running round, A.; Circumcursio.

@AC 3088. "The servant ran to meet her" (Genesis 24:17) = exploration from Divine good. Explained.

@3127. "The girl ran" (verse 28) = the propensity or animus of that affection.

@3131. "Laban ran out unto the man" (verse 29) = the animus of the affection of good towards truth.

@3804. "She ran and told her father" (Genesis 29:12) = acknowledgement through interior truths. To "run and tell" = the affection of making known; here, from acknowledgement.

@3806. "He ran to meet him" (verse 13) = agreement.

@4050[3]. Those of this province run about hither and thither.

@4350. "Esau ran to meet him" (Genesis 33:4) = the influx of Divine natural good.

@5135[10]. To "run with a thief" (Psalms 50:18) = to alienate truth from one's self through falsity.

@AR 437. To "run to war" (Revelation 9:9) = the ardour of fighting. @AE 558.

@SE 436. On punishment by a running round.

@AE 652[5]. To "run about" (Nahum 2:4) = to go astray.

Rupture.; Hernia. @CL 253.

Rush. See <Bulrush>.

Rush.; Ruere.
Rush in. Rush on.; Irruere.

@AC 587[end]. The whole human race is evil, and, from himself, everyone rushes to Hell.

@868. An Angel... from himself, would rush to Hell.

@929[end]. If a man were left to himself, he would rush into all evil and falsity.

@987[2]. If not hindered by external bonds... a man would rush into every crime... @4459[3]. @4793[2].

